<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco de Lacerda e Almeida</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1753–1798</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Francisco José de Lacerda e Almeida</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco José de Lacerda e Almeida was a Brazilian-born Portuguese mathematician and explorer. After surveying the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, he was appointed governor of the Rivers of Sena in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and in 1798 led an expedition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tete">Tete</a> towards the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazembe">Kazembe</a>, hoping to open a route across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>. He reached Kazembe's capital but died there of fever in October 1798, and his diary became an early source on the region.</p></div>
